 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** The server owns the socket layer and pushes each committed revision to connected browsers. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for its repeated reads and multi-second lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected for recurring fees and provider coupling. Owning the connection takes on capacity, reconnect, and catch-up work in exchange for fast updates and no dependency on a realtime vendor. The change store, delivery, and browser sync nodes are designed around replayable revisions, reliable publication, and socket sessions. See [decisions/change-delivery-transport.md](decisions/change-delivery-transport.md) for the full decision.
